  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 149 PM MST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\n\nSaturday will be fairly interesting as the day goes on. To start the \nmorning it will be windy as the 700mb jet slowly starts moving south \nacross the Colorado border. Our wind prone areas along I-80 and I-25 \nwill continue to have high winds to borderline highs through the \nmorning and early afternoon we will slowly see the winds start to \ndrop in the afternoon hours as a cold front shifts the winds from \ntheir westerly direction to a more northerly direction after the \ncold front passes through. While the winds will still be rather \nbreezy into the overnight hours, the possibility of high winds \nsignificantly decreases after 21z or 2pm Saturday. A mid-level open \nwave will also be pushing through Saturday morning on top of our \nHigh winds. While this system doesn't appear to be totally saturated \nthere are some plumes of moisture that will be able to produce some \nsnowfall from the synoptic forcing alone. Albeit the only places \nthat are going to see the snowfall in our high wind westerly pattern \nwill be the mountain peaks for Saturday morning and early afternoon. \nHowever, the cold front will be the deciding factor for everywhere \nexpected to pass through the forecast area by the late afternoon. A \nstrong vorticity gradient is associated with the cold front. The \nvorticity gradient is fairly strong enhancing the rain and snowfall \nrates. Areas east of I-25 will see a faster transition from rain to \nsnow as the cold front will reach them first as its expected to kind \nof pinwheel are the Laramie range as well as having less downsloping \nwinds compared to the Lower elevations west of the I-25 corridor. \nThe biggest problem for the lower elevations (I-25 and east) will be \novercoming the dry boundary layer set in place from the downsloping \nwinds off the breaking mountain wave. Another factor for the lower \nelevations will be how fast the precipitation will change over from \nrain to snow. With 700mb temperatures dropping down to -10c behind \nthe cold front, the transition should be fairly quick but getting \nthe boundary layer to reach near saturation would be needed before \nany precip can reach the ground. This cold airmass behind the cold \nfront unfortunately wont stay over us long as an upper level ridge \nwill push into the area Sunday advecting in warmer air into the \nregion. By Monday morning the lower elevations will be in the upper \n40's to lower 50's and the higher elevations will be in the mid to \nupper 40's. These warmer temperatures are expected to continue \nthrough mid-week and likely melting any snow that accumulated that \nis not on the mountain top.",
